        type: "concept",
        title: "Creating Names: Three Ways, Two Scopes, One Manager",
        content: `There are **three ways** to create a named range in Excel. The fastest: select a cell or range, click the **Name Box** (left of the formula bar), type a name, and press Enter. Done.

The second way: select the range, go to **Formulas → Define Name**. This opens a dialog where you can set the **scope**. The third way: select a range that has labels in its first row or column, then **Formulas → Create from Selection** — Excel auto-generates names from the labels.

**Scope** is critical. A name can be **workbook-scoped** (works from any sheet — most common) or **sheet-scoped** (only works on one specific sheet). Sheet-scope lets you reuse the same name on different sheets — useful when you have parallel structures (a Sales sheet and a Returns sheet both with a "Total" name).

The **Name Manager** (**Ctrl + F3** or Formulas → Name Manager) is mission control. It shows every name, its scope, its current value, and what it refers to. From here you can **edit**, **delete**, and **filter** names. It's the first place pros look when debugging a broken formula.`,
